ic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury officials have said repeatedly that they believe diplomacy will be far more effective in getting China to reform than trade sanctions. Last week, U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson led a delegation including almost half of President George W. Bush's cabinet for talks with senior Chinese officials on economic relations between the two countries.  \par \par \uc2 The meeting yielded some modest agreements, such as for sharing environmental and energy technology and pledges to work on an agreement protecting investment flows. At the close of the meeting, Paulson said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue will remain "at the core" of U.S.-China engagement.  \par \par \uc2 In the report released Tuesday, Treasury noted China's average monthly accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves was $20.4 billion in the first half this year. That measure fell to $15.6 billion in the third quarter, coinciding with a pick-up in the pace of yuan appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 The burden of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has hindered its ability to control interest rates and inflation, Treasury said. It has also impeded rebalancing of Chinese growth away from investment and exports in favor of more consumption, a stated goal of the Chinese government.  \par \par \uc2 For its part, the U.S. should help reduce global trade imbalances, which economists warn could spark financial market instability, by encouraging its citizens to save more and by cutting budget deficits.  \par \par \uc2 Turning to other trading partners, Treasury said tepid economic growth in Europe and Japan has contributed to global trade imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 "Europe and Japan each need to achieve higher rates of growth of domestic demand on a sustained basis," Treasury said, noting that it is uncertain whether Europe's brighter economic outlook will last. Growth in Japan is already decelerating from last year's recovery, Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 Most important U.S. trading partners have floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes in place. Japan, whose trade-weighted yen is at its weakest level in two decades, hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since March 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Malaysia - noted in previous Treasury reports for its large and growing current account surplus - like other oil-exporting countries would benefit from more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility, Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 Trade surpluses among major oil exporters are now outpacing those of Southeast Asia's developing economies, inflated by higher oil prices. Russia's current account surplus totaled $56.5 billion in the first half 2006, or equal to just over 11% of gross domestic product. \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage the pace of ruble appreciation against the dollar, 6% in the first half 2006.  \par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabia, like other Gulf countries, pegs its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar. Between 2004 and 2006, Saudi's oil revenue almost doubled to just under $200 billion. A portion of this money returns to the U.S. for investment, raising the U.S. capital account surplus, a mirror image of the current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury said the oil producers could help sustain global economic growth and reduce global trade imbalances by investing more in oil production capacity, investing more in their economies, and moving to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 19-12-06 2100GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Adds comment from Sen. Baucus)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Elizabeth Price \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--The U.S. Treasury Department on Tuesday again declined to designate China as guilty of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation in a report, noting the government in Beijing is moving steadily, though slowly, toward a flexible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Department of Treasury concluded that no major trading partner of the U.S. met the technical requirements for designation," according to the closely watched and overdue report to Congress on international exchange rate policies during the first half of 2006.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury outlined steps China is taking to loosen restrictions on capital moving in and out of the country and develop new financial instruments, such as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  futures, to prepare for a freely floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In recent months, the government has allowed the value of the yuan to fluctuate more and the pace of appreciation has picked up. This progress made it difficult to say that China is managing its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime with the intent of gaining a trade advantage, a senior Treasury official told reporters on condition of anonymity.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nonetheless, China is moving too cautiously and the pace of reforms is too slow, Treasury said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "China's cautious approach to exchange rate reform continues to exacerbate distortions in the domestic economy and impede adjustment of international imbalances," Treasury said. "Clamping down on exchange rate fluctuations has increased pressures in other parts of the economy."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury's decision, originally due Oct. 15, was widely expected. The department has refused to call China a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator in recent reports. And since the last report, released in May, China has allowed the yuan to appreciate more rapidly. The yuan strengthened 0.5% against the U.S. dollar in the second half of 2005 and another 1.0% in the first half 2006. Since China abandoned the dollar peg in July 2005, the yuan has risen just under 6.0%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This is far too little, say U.S. manufacturers and their representatives in Congress, who argue the yuan is undervalued by as much as 40%. Indeed, China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to accumulate massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves as a result of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent faster yuan appreciation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury will be under fire from those critics, who say the Bush administration hasn't been tough enough on China, when Congress returns for a new session in January. They got a boost last week when Federal Reserve Board Chairman Ben Bernanke, in the prepared text of a speech, called the undervalued yuan an export "subsidy."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Members from both the House and the Senate are working on legislation to overhaul the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reporting requirement and develop new laws, mostly directed at China, to pressure countries that limit \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation. A measure backed by Sens. Max Baucus, D-Mont., and Charles Grassley, R-Iowa, would target \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "misalignment" rather than "manipulation," taking the question of intent out of consideration of whether Treasury should call out a country for problematic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think that this report is a policy tool that has seen its day," Baucus said in a statement Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The issue is not whether China, Japan, or any other country is branded a 'manipulator'," Baucus added. "Rather, the issue is about getting our trading partners to use economic policies that are sustainable, fair and market-based."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury officials have said repeatedly that they believe diplomacy will be far more effective in getting China to reform, than trade sanctions. Last week, U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson led a delegation including almost half of President George W. Bush's cabinet for talks with senior Chinese officials on economic relations between the two countries.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The meeting yielded some modest agreements, such as for sharing environmental and energy technology and pledges to work on an agreement protecting investment flows. At the close of the meeting, Paulson said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issue will remain "at the core" of U.S.-China engagement.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the report released Tuesday, Treasury noted China's average monthly accumulation of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves was $20.4 billion in the first half this year. That measure fell to $15.6 billion in the third quarter, coinciding with a pick-up in the pace of yuan appreciation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The burden of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has hindered its ability to control interest rates and inflation, Treasury said. It has also impeded rebalancing of Chinese growth away from investment and exports in favor of more consumption, a stated goal of the Chinese government.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For its part, the U.S. should help reduce global trade imbalances, which economists warn could spark financial market instability, by encouraging its citizens to save more and by cutting budget deficits.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turning to other trading partners, Treasury said tepid economic growth in Europe and Japan has contributed to global trade imbalances.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Europe and Japan each need to achieve higher rates of growth of domestic demand on a sustained basis," Treasury said, noting that it is uncertain whether Europe's brighter economic outlook will last. Growth in Japan is already decelerating from last year's recovery, Treasury said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most important U.S. trading partners have floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regimes in place. Japan, whose trade-weighted yen is at its weakest level in two decades, hasn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets since March 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia - noted in previous Treasury reports for its large and growing current account surplus - like other oil-exporting countries would benefit from more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flexibility, Treasury said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trade surpluses among major oil exporters are now outpacing those of Southeast Asia's developing economies, inflated by higher oil prices. Russia's current account surplus totaled $56.5 billion in the first half 2006, or equal to just over 11% of gross domestic product. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to manage the pace of ruble appreciation against the dollar, which has gained 6% in the first half of 2006.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Saudi Arabia, like other Gulf countries, pegs its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the U.S. dollar. Between 2004 and 2006, Saudi's oil revenue almost doubled to just under $200 billion. A portion of this money returns to the U.S. for investment, raising the U.S. capital account surplus, a mirror image of the current account deficit.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury said the oil producers could help sustain global economic growth and reduce global trade imbalances by investing more in oil production capacity, investing more in their economies and moving to a more flexible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com [ 19-12-06 2153GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061219e2cj0017w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
