\par\par \b \uc2 Russia's dollar buying risks inflation-Greenspan  \b0\par\par\uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2006\par 20:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 19 (Reuters) - Russia should be wary about the inflationary impact of buying U.S. dollars to insulate its economy from an influx of foreign earnings from oil exports, former Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Russia, the world's second largest oil exporter, has been earning about $600 million a day from its oil and gas exports, pushing its rouble \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher and threatening to cause "Dutch Disease".  \par \par \uc2 Dutch Disease refers to the potential negative long-term impact of one explosive sector -- oil, in the case of Russia -- which boosts the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , making locally produced goods less competitive compared to foreign goods.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan warned that the standard cure for Dutch Disease -- \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- could increase the monetary base without the necessary infrastructure to neutralize the move's impact on inflation.  \par \par \uc2 "Unless you're able to sterilize these (foreign) assets, it's going to be a struggle," Greenspan told the Fifth Annual Investor Conference on \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by buying U.S. dollars to slow the rouble's rise. The move, however, fuelled money supply growth of about 45 percent and inflation of around 10.9 percent in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 The term Dutch Disease was coined to describe the decline of the manufacturing sector in the Netherlands after the discovery of natural gas in the 1960s.  \par \par \uc2 Russia has about $268 billion in reserves, the third highest after Japan and China. The country's reserves have grown 54 percent this year due to record high oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 The rouble has risen 4.3 percent to the dollar/euro basket this year.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan said the creation of Russia's stabilization fund has helped the country "combat Dutch Disease" because the fund is "insulated from the political system."  \par \par \uc2 When the price of oil exceeds $27 per barrel, the extra revenues from oil and gas are channeled to a budget stabilization fund aimed at financing social welfare programs and helping during emergencies.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan also discussed the prospects of the rouble as a reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  similar to the dollar and euro.  \par \par \uc2 The rouble is "still far from being a reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the dollar and euro should be extremely liquid," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan added that for the rouble to be an "external \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 " it is important that the "rule of law" prevails.  \par \par \uc2 "People would want to invest in a country where they feel their money would be safe. In the United States, we have worked on that for over 200 years. It doesn't happen overnight," Greenspan said.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-RUSSIA-GREENSPAN|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|U|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061019e2aj002lo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
