\par\par \b \uc2 Eyeing Georgia, Russia mulls money transfer ban  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gleb Bryanski  \par \uc2 519 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2006\par 19:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Oct 2 (Reuters) - Russia's parliament said on Monday it will debate a draft law that would allow officials to ban cross-border money transfers, a measure clearly directed at ex-Soviet neighbour Georgia.  \par \par \uc2 The decision followed the arrest last week by Georgia of four Russian army officers on spying charges, triggering the worst crisis in years between the ex-Soviet neighbours.  \par \par \uc2 The four officers were allowed to fly back to \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by international mediators.  \par \par \uc2 "This was a provocation and the State Duma deputies must respond to it," Boris Gryzlov, speaker of the lower house of parliament, told reporters. "I think we will prepare and submit this draft law on Wednesday."  \par \par \uc2 Any measure that would make it harder for Georgian workers in Russia to send money home could be a huge blow for their country's economy.  \par \par \uc2 About one million Georgians work in Russia. They sent home $219 million in the first half of 2006, or 14.6 percent of gross domestic product, according to estimates by Georgia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Vladislav Reznik, head of the Duma's banking committee, said the draft law would give the government powers to limit or ban cross-border money transfers.  \par \par \uc2 "We believe the government must have this right but whether it uses it or not, the government can decide for itself," Reznik told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 The Duma is controlled by the pro-government United Russia faction and normally rubber stamps legislation supported by President Vladimir Putin.  \par \par \uc2 Russia last changed its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in July this year, lifting controls on capital \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements in a move to make the rouble a convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 CHASING OIL WEALTH  \par \par \uc2 Millions of workers from ex-Soviet states work in Russia, where windfall oil revenues have been driving a rise in incomes. Many do jobs Russians are not prepared to do, such as cleaning streets, repairing apartments and manning market stands.  \par \par \uc2 The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  estimates migrant workers from ex-Soviet states sent $3.2 billion to their home countries in 2005 and the amount is expected to grow further.  \par \par \uc2 Money transfers have also become a booming business and dozens of newcomers, including state-owned giant Sberbank and Russian Post as well as many smaller firms, compete with industry veterans like Western Union.  \par \par \uc2 Russian Post said on Monday it would temporarily freeze all money transfers to Georgia from Russia by mail. It cited unsatisfactory work by their Georgian counterparts. Tbilisi said any ban on money transfers would have little effect on Georgia's economy.  \par \par \uc2 "We can use the banks of other countries," said Nika Rurua, deputy head of the security and defence committee in the Georgian parliament. Moscow slapped a ban on imports of Georgian wines and bottled water in early 2006 while on Monday, Russia's transport ministry ordered air, rail, land and sea links to Georgia to be suspended. (Additional reporting by Guy Faulconbridge, Ivan Rodin and Vladimir Kuznetsov)  \par \par \uc2 RUSSIA-GEORGIA-REMITTANCES|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|G|BNX|RNP|PGE  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061002e2a2001n6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
