\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Russia ready to raise rates, reserve requirements  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Douglas Busvine  \par \uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2006\par 09:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Writes through with full news conference)  \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Aug 25 (Reuters) - Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it was ready to raise interest rates and tighten banking reserve requirements to curb inflation, but was still confident of meeting its 2006 price and exchange rate targets.  \par \par \uc2 First Deputy Chairman Alexei Ulyukayev also told reporters the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had submitted for parliamentary review its monetary policy guidelines for 2007 -- which foresee a reduction in inflation -- without significant changes.  \par \par \uc2 "We are sticking to our inflation target for this year -- the figure of 8.5 percent remains unchanged," Ulyukayev told a news conference.  \par \par \uc2 Inflation was 9.3 percent in the 12 months to July, but Ulyukayev forecast a fall of 0.1 percent in consumer prices in the month of August. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's full-year inflation goal is below the government's own 9.0 percent forecast.  \par \par \uc2 He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also keeping its 9.0 percent target for the rouble's real effective appreciation -- a measure of competitiveness which accounts for differences in inflation with Russia's trading partners.  \par \par \uc2 The twin policy targets, criticised by many economists as contradictory, have come under pressure this year as Russia, now the world's largest petroleum producer, harvests a massive windfall from near-record oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to take out some of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's half a billion dollars a day in oil export revenues, seeking to prevent a sharp rise in the rouble which would hurt the competitiveness of domestic businesses.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  floods the local money market with rouble liquidity and, with the banking system still unable to channel all that cash into investment, much of it is going into pumping up the prices of goods, services and assets.  \par \par \uc2 LESSER EVIL  \par \par \uc2 Next year's monetary policy guidelines, which see inflation at between 6.0 and 8.5 percent and allow for real effective rouble appreciation of zero to 10 percent, give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more wiggle room on the exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 "Growth in the nominal effective exchange rate is a lesser evil than high inflation," said Ulyukayev, adding the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  considered fighting inflation its primary task.  \par \par \uc2 The rouble has risen by about four percent in nominal terms this year against a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket comprising $0.6 and 0.4 euros, an \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  guide used by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which irons out some of the volatility in the dollar-euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair.  \par \par \uc2 Ulyukayev said there were no plans for now to alter the composition of the basket. "We are examining the theoretical possibility of changing the basket, but in general we are happy with the current composition," he told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 POLICY OPTIONS  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come under fire from politicians -- most recently President Vladimir Putin -- for allowing the rouble to rise. That has forced it to experiment with other measures to contend with inflation.  \par \par \uc2 It has raised its short-term deposit rates three times this year to 2.0-2.5 percent, although they remain negative in real terms and have failed to put a floor under the money market.  \par \par \uc2 Also this week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the minimum reserve requirements on liabilities of banks towards non-resident banks, in a step aimed at curbing rapid growth in foreign borrowing by the banks and corporates.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say that a wide current account surplus, external borrowing by the private sector, foreign direct investment and speculative inflows after Russia's recent abolition of capital controls are all putting upward pressure on the rouble.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't rule out making use of minimum reserve requirements, but we don't have any concrete plans," Ulyukayev said. "The same applies to deposit rates. We don't rule out raising them in the future."  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-RUSSIA-CBANK (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|BNX|ABX|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060825e28p000k9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
