\par\par \b \uc2 Russia's Kudrin: Strict Policy Can Contain Ruble -Agency  \b0\par\par\uc2 173 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2006\par 17:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW (Dow Jones)--The Russian government will be able to keep the ruble from appreciating if it conducts strict monetary policy, Finance Minister Alexei Kudrin said at a meeting with President Vladimir Putin Wednesday, news agency Prime-Tass reported Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Strict monetary policy includes continuing to accumulate the federal budget's extra revenues from progressive oil export taxes in the Stabilization Fund and continuing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Kudrin said.  \par \par \uc2 The Russian ruble gained 7.9% in real terms in January-July, the CBR said Tuesday. The figure is based on the real effective rate, a trade-weighted index that gauges the appreciation or depreciation of the ruble against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia's major trade partners taking inflation into account.  \par \par \uc2 The ruble rose 10.9% against the dollar in January-July in real terms and 5.7% against the euro, the CBR said.  \par \par \uc2 News agency Web site:  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.prime-tass.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.prime-tass.com }}}\uc2  [ 09-08-06 1541GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060809e289000zy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
