\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Putin picks Ignatyev for second Russia c.bank term  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Douglas Busvine  \par \uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2005\par 11:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with lawmaker, paras 5-6, adds background)  \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Nov 3 (Reuters) - President Vladimir Putin has nominated Sergei Ignatyev for a second four-year term as chairman of the Bank of Russia, the presidential press service said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Ignatyev, a low-profile technocrat who has also held senior finance ministry posts, has steered the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  through an oil-fuelled boom during which inflation has gradually fallen but stayed stubbornly above single-digit rates.  \par \par \uc2 He is widely seen as a steadying hand and a welcome contrast to his predecessor Viktor Gerashchenko, who headed the bank for two turbulent spells before his final dismissal in 2002.  \par \par \uc2 "It is clear from this appointment that Ignatyev has successfully carried out what the Kremlin wanted him to do. Monetary policy under Ignatyev was fairly balanced," said Peter Westin, chief economist at MDM Bank in Moscow.  \par \par \uc2 A lawmaker said Ignatyev's candidacy should quickly win parliamentary confirmation, although some members of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board may be replaced in spring 2006.  \par \par \uc2 "Ignatyev is an exceptionally professional \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chairman. Any banking system would be proud of him," Vladislav Reznik, head of the State Duma's finance committee, was quoted by Prime-Tass news agency as saying.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said, however, that Ignatyev has had to work within political constraints that have complicated his task of defending price stability.  \par \par \uc2 "We haven't seen him being somebody who can stand up and protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's opinion against political influence," said Alexei Moisseev, head of fixed income research at Renaissance Capital.  \par \par \uc2 Soon after winning re-election last year, Putin instructed Ignatyev to keep the rouble exchange rate low to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic competitiveness. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  obliged, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and inflation rose as a result.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eventually yielded to speculators betting on a stronger rouble, but inflation has since failed to respond and the authorities will struggle this year to improve on the final 2004 figure of 11.7 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to say, 'Inflation is a problem and we're going to fight it and we don't care if that hurts this or that producer ... We are independent,'" said Moisseev.  \par \par \uc2 STABILITY RESTORED  \par \par \uc2 Under Ignatyev's stewardship, Russia recovered from the aftermath of its 1998 devaluation and default on $40 billion in domestic debts.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated the largest foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of any country outside Asia, now a record $164 billion, as oil export revenues flood into the country.  \par \par \uc2 While its dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has prevented the rouble <RUB=> from turning into an overvalued petro-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there have been economic side-effects.  \par \par \uc2 Money supply is expanding at an annual rate of over 40 percent. Interest rates are far below inflation, hurting savers and sending mixed signals to investors.  \par \par \uc2 And, in real terms, Russia has lost the competitive edge it won in the 1998 crash, putting pressure on Ignatyev to focus more on beating inflation and less on the exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 "The problem now is how to deal with the dual target -- inflation and rouble exchange rate. Towards the end of this year or at the beginning of next year we should see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaning towards targeting inflation," said Westin.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has won praise for strengthening Russia's banking system, introducing a deposit insurance scheme and killing off non-viable "pocket" banks.  \par \par \uc2 But the banking system remains vulnerable to shocks, as a run on secondary lenders in summer 2004 showed. And Russia still has over 1,200 banks, a number that must fall for the financial sector to become a a motor of economic development.  \par \par \uc2 "Ignatyev should also show initiative and speed up the banking sector reform," said Westin. (Additional reporting by Gleb Bryanski and Meg Clothier)  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-RUSSIA-CBANK (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|G|BNX|RNP|PGE  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051103e1b3000l1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
