\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Russia takes steps to curb rouble demand.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Darya Korsunskaya  \par \uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2004\par 16:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Nov 26 (Reuters) - Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  acted on Friday to reduce upward pressure on the rouble , trading near four-year highs to the dollar, by cutting the amount of roubles exporters are required to purchase.  \par \par \uc2 Russia will lower the percentage of revenues exporters must switch from hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to roubles to 10 percent from 25 percent, Deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chairman Konstantin Korishchenko said.  \par \par \uc2 The decision, which will mean exporters are committed to buying fewer roubles with their dollars, will come into force when published in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official bulletin, normally issued on Thursdays, Korishchenko told a financial conference.  \par \par \uc2 Exports topped $16 billion in both September and August, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data. The rouble has surged on receipts from booming oil exports.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the move was also aimed at trumpeting confidence that there was no danger of another rouble collapse like one in 1998 which destroyed Russians' savings.  \par \par \uc2 "It demonstrates that they do not see any problems with dollar supply and no risks for rouble weakness," said ING analyst Julia Tsepliaeva.  \par \par \uc2 Tsepliaeva estimated, however, that firms repatriate as much as 60 percent of their export earnings, suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move may be "psychological than practical".  \par \par \uc2 Roubles for "tomorrow" delivery last traded down on the previous session's fix at 28.3375 to the dollar on the MICEX exchange, giving up earlier near four-year highs, shadowing moves on international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 The rouble has become highly sensitive to moves in the euro-dollar as sophisticated speculative money is playing a bigger role in the market.  \par \par \uc2 Moscow's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange said it plans to create a new rouble/euro contract in early 2005 to make it easier to trade euros, lending more depth to Russia's dollar-driven \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 KEEP COMPETING  \par \par \uc2 Russia must counteract upward pressure on the rouble if its fledgling non-energy sector is to stay competitive, as it aims to have a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2007.  \par \par \uc2 At the moment the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dampens rouble appreciation by periodically buying dollars - $11 billion in October - at artificially high prices, but economists warn this exacerbates inflation, one of Russia's most enduring economic problems.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday First Deputy Chairman Alexei Ulyukayev said if the bank did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  the rouble could surge to 20 to the dollar, which would damage the economy, so the bank would continue to play a major role in the market during 2005.  \par \par \uc2 Korishchenko said the rouble would become more volatile next year as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  completes a shift in its exchange rate policy from targeting the dollar to focusing on a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  now uses a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket weighted at least 60 percent in euros and at least 30 percent in dollars to guide its exchange rate policy.  \par \par \uc2 "What we are seeing on the market will disappear, and the market will behave more flexibly," he said. "This will allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  also hopes that its plans to convert a budget stabilisation fund of surplus oil revenues into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would also reduce pressure on the rouble, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 At the moment the fund - set to top 500 billion roubles ($17.5 billion) this year - is held in interest-bearing rouble accounts, but the government will move the cash into top-rated investments in dollars, euros and sterling in early 2005.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041126e0bq009c7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
