\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Russia  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  policy seen helping euro one day.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak and James Thornhill  \par \uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2004\par 17:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 18 (Reuters) - Russia signalled on Thursday a greater role for the euro in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, lending support to the belief that Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is gaining credibility and demand from world central banks.  \par \par \uc2 This spells out potentially greater support for the euro in the long term as monetary authorities, including Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buy euros to replace some of the dollars in their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said it was not clear how soon or to what extent Russia would boost the proportion of euros in its coffers, after Deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chairman Konstantin Korishchenko said the proportion of the euro in the current basket that helps guide the value of the rouble was bigger than many analysts expected.  \par \par \uc2 But many believed a continued rise in the euro part of the reserves was inevitable.  \par \par \uc2 "The fact that they are telling us that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket will have a much higher proportion of the euro means that their reserves will have a higher proportion of the euro," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS in London.  \par \par \uc2 "This is a likely scenario. And it is supportive of the euro."  \par \par \uc2 The proportion of euros in Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of more than $100 billion is 25-30 percent, up from 10 percent two years ago, Korishchenko told Reuters in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 JOINING THE BANDWAGON  \par \par \uc2 Russia's reserve breakdown compares with data from the International Monetary Fund's annual report which shows that global central banks hold about two thirds of their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in U.S. dollars and 19.7 percent in euros.  \par \par \uc2 In 1999, the euro's first year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  took up 13.5 percent of official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Other central banks are also expected to beef up their euro holdings as\par the dollar remains mired in a multi-year downtrend. On Thursday, the\par dollar hit a record low at $1.3074 per euro  and a nine-year low versus\par a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2        .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But little information is available about the extent of their plans for future euro purchases. And analysts said Russia's comments were also too difficult to interpret.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 60 plus percent, the euro's share in the basket used by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to guide the value of the rouble is much bigger than the bank's euro reserves.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But analysts said it was not clear if Russia would seek to bring the level of reserves up to match the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket, even though Korishchenko said this heavy weighting was derived from the high proportion of Russia's trade with countries linked in some way to the euro.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At current rates, Russia would have to buy roughly $30 billion worth of euros in order to bring its euro portion of reserves up to 60 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But many analysts believed Russia's heavy debt servicing costs denominated in dollars were an argument to keep the euro reserves lower.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In terms of external debt structure, that is roughly 1/3 in euros and the rest in dollars, but they confused everyone by saying reserve composition would also reflect trade weightings, which would mean a higher proportion of euros," said Dwyfor Evans, strategist at Bank of America in London.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They haven't been particularly clear, in fact they've probably been deliberately ambiguous. They're not going to tell the market what they're going to do on reserves.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The only thing we can say is there is likely to be more euro buying from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Korishchenko's comments appeared to back this view, many analysts said. "Our decisions are based on several factors including foreign debt structure, the structure of our foreign trade and so on," the central banker said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PSYCHOLOGICAL WORK  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another issue is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been coming to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market recently to buy dollars and depress the appreciating rouble in order to protect its export competitiveness.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said this was unlikely to change.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But rhetoric such as Thursday's comments from Korishchenko was likely aimed at increasing the euro's role as a transaction \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Russia which is struggling to make its economy less dollarised.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's mainly psychological - they're making it more explicit that they're targeting the basket and maybe hope that people will start trading against the basket rather than just the dollar," said Giancarlo Perasso, head of emerging markets research at WestLB. "It's a buffer against dollar weakness."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041118e0bi00b6z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
