\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Russia to hit '04 growth target, overshoot on CPI.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Meg Clothier  \par \uc2 376 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2004\par 16:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Oct 4 (Reuters) - Russia will hit government growth targets this year but overshoot its inflation goal, spurred by high oil prices and soaring factory-gate costs, a Reuters survey of 16 economists showed on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 For a table of survey findings, click here.  \par \par \uc2 The rouble will finish the year marginally weaker at 29.34 to the dollar, with further softening seen next year, according to a mean average of forecasts.  \par \par \uc2 "We think that under the conditions of high oil prices, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue the policy of capping the rouble's real exchange rate, whereas the task of lowering inflation will remain secondary," CSFB analyst Sergei Voloboev said.  \par \par \uc2 The twin - and contradictory - planks of Russia's monetary policy are to cap inflation at 10 percent and real effective rouble appreciation at 7 percent this year.  \par \par \uc2 The analysts polled saw inflation at 10.9 percent this year, with consumer prices set to grow 0.3 percent month-on-month in September.  \par \par \uc2 Latest monthly CPI figures are due on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "There are price pressures across the board," said Julia Ovanessian, an economist at JP Morgan.  \par \par \uc2 With no parliamentary and presidential elections due anytime soon, the government may ease back on curbing service prices, she said. High oil costs and a lower-than-usual seasonal drop in fruit and vegetable prices also played a role.  \par \par \uc2 Producer price inflation would hit 24.7 percent this year, nearly double the 2003 figure, the poll showed.  \par \par \uc2 However, the government is likely to meet its growth target, with economists predicting 7 percent growth this year.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves will top $100 billion by the end of this year, climbing to nearly $120 billion by the end of 2005, the survey showed.  \par \par \uc2 Driving the increase is \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb rouble strength and keep \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy competitive, as well as heavy borrowing by corporates looking to invest and refinance existing debt on better terms.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves continue to grow at a fast pace because of high global oil and metal prices and because Russian banks and companies are acquiring loans in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Trust analyst Michael Workman said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041004e0a400dwc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
