\par\par \b \uc2 Russia sees Sept capital inflow, $12 bln 04 outflow.  \b0\par\par\uc2 305 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2004\par 13:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Sept 30 (Reuters) - Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday forecast the country's first capital inflow in many months for September but forecast net outflow of $12 billion for 2004, almost six times 2003's figure.  \par \par \uc2 "In September, we are expecting not an outflow but an inflow of capital of between $500 million and $1 billion and this tells us that a $12 billion figure, which we and the government have lately given, is realistic for 2004," \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  First Deputy Chairman Alexei Ulyukayev told the parliamentary budget committee.  \par \par \uc2 Ulyukayev also revised the first half capital outflow forecast upwards to $7.5 billion from an earlier forecast for $5.5 billion but did not give any reason.  \par \par \uc2 Last year $2.3 billion of investment capital left Russia, the lowest since the collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Deputy Prime Minister Alexander Zhukov said high prices for oil, the country's key export, were behind this year's increase in capital outflows which he forecast at between $10 billion and $15 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Russian oil companies tend to keep their earnings in dollars rather than repatriate them, meaning earnings from high oil prices do not appear as capital inflows.  \par \par \uc2 Zhukov also said expectations of rising U.S. interest rates and falling yields on different Russian instruments were prompting more capital to leave the country.  \par \par \uc2 Many economists blame a recent crisis of confidence in the banking sector and the troubles besetting oil major YUKOS (YUKO.RTS) for the rise in net capital outflows.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  manages the exchange rate, using \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the rouble turning into a petro-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But the rouble liquidity resulting from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces down interest rates, with the U.S. Fed funds rate acting as a floor.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040930e09u008sz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
