\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Higher rouble rates to hurt short-sellers - cbank.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Artyom Danielyan  \par \uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2004\par 14:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Sept 6 (Reuters) - Russian money market rates will take their lead from abroad and hold above recent near-zero levels, making it harder to short-sell the rouble, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Chairman Konstantin Korishchenko said on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Korishchenko told Reuters in an interview that, with short-term rates rising, it will not be easy for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders to turn a profit by borrowing roubles to fund long dollar positions.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  manages the rouble's float, limiting its control over short-term domestic interest rates. The money market is therefore driven by foreign interest rates and liquidity created by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars, which is aimed at neutralising \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive inflows of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to the high value of its energy exports.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight rates were near zero from last December through to March, but rose during a recent financial crisis which forced eight Russian banks to close, and have been underpinned by recent tightening by the U.S. Federal Reserve.  \par \par \uc2 "All this speculation as to whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise its (dollar) bid by 3-5 kopecks or not is only all right for those who have no liquidity problems," Korishchenko said.  \par \par \uc2 "I do not think it is a very sensible strategy to try to establish long dollar positions to the detriment of the goal of managing liquidity."  \par \par \uc2 Korishchenko's comments appeared aimed at sowing uncertainty to compensate for flaws in Russia's policy of targeting both inflation and the exchange rate which create ample opportunities for \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders to turn a profit.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has set a target for the real effective appreciation of the rouble this year of seven percent, has been regularly seen bidding on the dollar to weaken the rouble.  \par \par \uc2 And, with Russian inflation likely to top an official target of 10 percent this year, analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to push the rouble's nominal value down further if it is to hit its exchange rate goal.  \par \par \uc2 The rouble has fallen by 2.5 percent to the dollar since March and traded at 29.2140 on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 END-MONTH SPIKE  \par \par \uc2 Higher Russian money market rates have been underpinned by two U.S. interest rate hikes this year, with analysts expecting a third quarter-percentage point hike to 1.75 percent at the next Fed meeting on Sept. 21.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight rate ended last month as high as 8-10 percent, as liquidity was drained by companies paying their monthly tax bills, and has eased in September only to 4-6 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "We are entering a phase when, it appears, rates will be significantly different from zero," Korishchenko said.  \par \par \uc2 "Since the situation has changed with rates starting to rise abroad, careful steering of liquidity is now on the agenda ... It is a general macro-economic process, beyond the scope of Russia. We must recognise it and carefully follow the trend that it sets," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040906e09600a3x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
