\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Russia not ready for rouble free float-cbank paper.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vlasta Demyanenko  \par \uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2004\par 18:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Aug 17 (Reuters) - Russia will continue to target both the exchange rate and inflation next year because its economy is not ready for a free-floating rouble, according to a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy paper obtained by Reuters on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 In its draft monetary policy guidelines for 2005, the Bank of Russia targets real effective appreciation of the rouble of 8 percent, up from 7 percent this year. It sees inflation at 6.5-8.5 percent, down from 10 percent in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 That implies a broadly stable nominal exchange rate for the rouble , which is widely seen as undervalued but whose appreciation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has curbed through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at maintaining \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with an open and insufficiently diversified economy highly dependent on the external financial and economic climate, is not yet able to move to a freely-floating rouble regime," the document said.  \par \par \uc2 The rouble has made good only a fraction of the losses it sustained in the 1998 financial collapse, enabling the economy to maintain rapid rates of growth led by a revival of oil and industrial production.  \par \par \uc2 But the twin-track policy of targeting both the exchange rate and inflation has risks, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would continue to prepare the ground for the rouble to float freely, enabling monetary policy to focus on taming inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Other planks of the strategy include tighter banking supervision in the wake of a mini-crisis which has forced eight lenders to close since May, and increased use of existing money market tools to fine-tune banking sector liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 SCENARIO PLANNING  \par \par \uc2 The strategy contains three scenarios: The first assumes an average price of $22.50 per barrel for Russia's Urals crude oil export blend, the second a price of $26 a barrel and the third a price of $31.  \par \par \uc2 Those scenarios do not tally, however, with the latest three-year outlook released on Monday by the Economy Ministry, which forecast oil at $28 a barrel and hiked its 2005 inflation outlook to 8-9 percent from 6.5-8.5 percent previously.  \par \par \uc2 Urals crude was was trading at $41.54 on Tuesday, as world prices hover near record highs. That spells a huge revenue windfall for Russia, the world's number two oil exporter, but also creates policy headaches as the authorities print money in their bid to stop the rouble turning into a petro-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 A source said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not received the revised Economy Ministry forecast - to be used in finalising the 2005 budget draft - when it was working on its projections.  \par \par \uc2 Depending on the scenario used by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Russia's oil-fuelled balance of payments surplus is seen at $19.7-$40.6 billion in 2005, after a projected $39.1 billion this year.  \par \par \uc2 It also forecasts an increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gold reserves of $6-$24.9 billion, compared with a forecast $23 billion gain in 2004.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have risen due to dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has in turn boosted the money supply. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its forecast for money supply growth to 34-38 percent this year from 25 percent.  \par \par \uc2 It forecast growth of demand for money of 20-35 percent next year but, with a reassurance that this would not translate into faster inflation, said the rate of circulation of money should slow.  \par \par \uc2 Net capital outflows are forecast at $4-$6 billion next year, compared with $6.7 billion this year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.  \par \par \uc2 Again, that appears out of date after the Economy Ministry recently raised its forecast of capital outflows this year to $8-$12 billion this year, driven largely by bank withdrawals by savers shaken by the recent banking turmoil.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040817e08h00cvz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
