\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Russian  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\uc2  To Ease Crisis  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Geoffrey T. Smith  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2004\par 19:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday to ease \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's worsening banking crisis, pumping liquidity into the system and arranging a bailout for the biggest bank to have run into trouble.  \par \par \uc2 Also, one of Russia's biggest private banks, Alfa Bank (ALFA.YY), moved to reassure the market that it has sufficient funds to meet its obligations after lines of depositors formed outside its branches. Alfa said it will meet all its obligations "in full and without delay."  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has halved the amount it requires retail and commercial banks to place on deposit with it in statutory reserve accounts, only three weeks after a smaller move failed to stop the spread of the country's most serious loss of confidence in its banks since 1998. The reserve requirement for all ruble deposits, and foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits held by corporate clients, will fall to 3.5% from 7% as of July 8.  \par \par \uc2 Cuts in the reserve requirement are one of the scarce tools effectively available to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , since few of the banks in trouble hold enough government-quality collateral to pledge against \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  credit. The only other way for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to inject liquidity would be to sell more rubles at its daily \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions, but that would threaten to expand the money supply and increase inflation rip, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 Separately, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it is brokering a deal for state-owned Vneshtorgbank, Russia's second-largest lender, to acquire ZAO Guta Bank, Russia's 22nd biggest bank by assets. Guta Bank closed its doors and stopped making payments Tuesday. It's the largest victim to date of a wholesale loss of confidence by Russia's small and medium-sized banks in each other.  \par \par \uc2 A more modest cut of 2 percentage points on reserve requirements two weeks ago freed over $1 billion for the interbank market, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  estimates. But that wasn't enough to solve the problems faced by banks such as Guta.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chairman Sergei Ignatyev said VTB's buy-out of Guta would be financed by a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  loan secured against VTB's portfolio of government bonds. Guta's shareholders said later they have accepted the proposal, while VTB said it expects talks over the details to continue until the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 The "rescue" of Guta comes at the price of nationalizing a bank which was technically solvent, inasmuch as its assets exceeded its liabilities. As such, the incident has spurred painful memories of Russia's ruthlessly aggressive corporate wars of the 1990s - all the more since it coincides with an intensification of what many believe to be the politically motivated assault on oil company OAO Yukos (YUKO.RS).  \par \par \uc2 It isn't clear from either bank's statements, or Ignatyev's, whether the solution to Guta's problems is intended as a temporary or a permanent one.  \par \par \uc2 The panic in the banking sector has evoked alarming memories of Russia's 1998 financial crash, when some of its biggest banks collapsed. Compared with six years ago, Russia's economy is much healthier and experts say there is no underlying cause for alarm. Ignatyev said the problems currently facing the industry are "psychological." "There is no crisis," he told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 The trouble started in May when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  revoked the license of OOO Sodbusinessbank for alleged money-laundering. Another smaller bank, CreditTrust, went into voluntary liquidation three weeks later after a run by depositors.  \par \par \uc2 Rumors then surfaced of a "blacklist" of other banks facing closure, leading to a crisis of confidence throughout the system. Interest rates surged on the interbank market, as banks effectively stopped lending to each other.  \par \par \uc2 Ignatyev said there was no blacklist, and that the country's biggest privately owned banks - Alfa Bank, MDM Bank and Rosbank - had no problems. The issue has so far not affected the country's larger banks, especially VTB and state-owned OAO Sberbank (SBER.RS).  \par \par \uc2 Ignatyev also said that a Russian commercial bank, which he didn't name, had identified itself as a possible buyer for AKB Dialog-Optim, a smaller bank that is also experiencing liquidity problems.  \par \par \uc2 Dialog-Optim declined to comment Wednesday but its sole shareholder, Alexander Polyakov, told journalists last Friday that he didn't want to sell.  \par \par \uc2 -By Geoffrey T. Smith, Dow Jones Newswires (+7 095) 974 8055; geoffrey.smith@dowjones.com [ 07-07-04 1702GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040707e077000s8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
