\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Momentum building for G7 expansion.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Dolan  \par \uc2 981 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2004\par 18:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 17 (Reuters) - Momentum seems to be building at last for the Group of Seven economic powers - the closest the global economy has to a governing council - to be expanded.  \par \par \uc2 Although economic experts and commentators have for years called for a reform of the Group's membership to better reflect shifts in world economic geography and power over the past decade, G7 members are now acknowledging the need for change.  \par \par \uc2 The issue of expanding the informal club, first started almost 30 years ago, was a key topic at last week's annual summit of the Group of Eight (G7 plus Russia).  \par \par \uc2 "It's about time there was some movement on this issue and we think it rather urgent now," said Jim O'Neill, Chief Global Economist at Goldman Sachs. "There are just two many pressing world economic issues that cannot be adequately addressed or resolved by such a narrow western dominated grouping."  \par \par \uc2 G7 has its roots in the mid-1970s, when economic crises resulting from the breakdown of the post-war Bretton Woods \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system and soaring oil prices led to regular gatherings of political and financial leaders from the United States, Japan, Germany, France, Britain, Italy and Canada.  \par \par \uc2 Genuinely representing the biggest economic powers back then, it rocked financial markets in the 1980s by sanctioning several successful bouts of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to control volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was added to the annual summits in the 1990s to make it G8, but the finance teams which focus on the pressing economic issues remain just the G7 and meet four times a year.  \par \par \uc2 On many measures, G7 is now a lopsided world grouping.  \par \par \uc2 Measuring 2003 economic output by adjusting for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's purchasing power puts the top seven powers more along of the lines of the U.S., China, the European Union, India, Russia Brazil and Japan. Last week, German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder confirmed Brazil, China and India had been mentioned as candidates for G7/G8. And Brazil's Foreign Minister Celso Amorim claimed France supported inviting the three to the table too.  \par \par \uc2 Canadian Prime Minister Paul Martin and Italian Prime Minister Silvio Berluconi, leaders of two countries who could have most to lose from a rebalancing of the Group to reflect global economic weight, talked openly of expanding the group.  \par \par \uc2 And only last month, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said China was likely to be invited to the next meeting of G7 finance ministers and central bankers in Washington in October.  \par \par \uc2 REFORM TO WHAT?  \par \par \uc2 Goldman's O'Neill and its Vice Chairman Robert Hormats, a U.S. G7 Deputy for the first eight G7 meetings, jointly penned an paper this month suggesting a number of expanded formats.  \par \par \uc2 O'Neill said the first and simplest step would be to add China. But he said a longer term proposal was for the creation of what Goldman called "F8" - this would be the U.S., Japan, China, Britain, Canada and three pan-European representatives for the euro zone.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, chairman of the euro group and European Monetary Commissioner already attend G7 meetings but not as full members. Germany, France and Italy retain the top seats at the table.  \par \par \uc2 "Getting the Europeans to consolidate representation may be a harder job than adding China," O'Neill said.  \par \par \uc2 But a suggestion that is gaining support from several quarters is elevating the role and scope of the wide and inclusive G20 grouping, which currently meets once at year at finance ministry level, and convening a summit of the group too.  \par \par \uc2 Canada advocates a bigger role for the G20 and premier Martin held out the possibility of a summit next year.  \par \par \uc2 "Canada's idea is gaining a lot of support. Is it for today? No. Could it be done at the beginning of next year? I think so. The possibility is improving," Martin said  \par \par \uc2 In a paper for Washington-based think-tank The Brookings Institution, former World Bank Vice President Johannes Linn said the G20 bloc, which includes developing giants such as China, India, Russia and Brazil as well as the G7, was the only appropriate bloc to represent a global economic council.  \par \par \uc2 "In a world where the G7/G8 economies still appear so dominant, it may be difficult to grasp the degree to which the global economy is today already multipolar," wrote Linn and co-author Colin Bradford, a former chief economist at the United States Agency for International Development (USAID).  \par \par \uc2 The G20 as a whole represents two thirds of the world's population, 90 percent of the world's gross national product and accounts for 80 percent of world trade.  \par \par \uc2 G20, chaired by Germany this year, consists of the G7, including European Union representatives, and Australia - alongside China, India, South Korea, Indonesia, Brazil, Mexico, Argentina, Russia, Turkey, South Africa and Saudi Arabia. Heads of the IMF and World Bank also take part.  \par \par \uc2 With China taking up the presidency of the group next year, it may well be the ideal opportunity to make the switch.  \par \par \uc2 Set up in Berlin in 1999 to deal with flawed market and institutional mechanisms that led to the Asian financial turmoil of 1997 and 1998, the G20's remit has widened since.  \par \par \uc2 O'Neill at Goldman said convening G20 summits on single issues would be a way forward and with its membership is ideal, for example, to restart the stalled Doha trade round next year. There is no reason why the G7 or F8 could not continue to meet more regularly around this event, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Others feel G7 and G8 may just have to wither away.  \par \par \uc2 "G7 is a relic of an old order that no longer exists," said Avinash Persaud, investment director at GAM London. "It can try to prolong its life by reforming, but ultimately it will be abolished in its current form."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040617e06h002i6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
