\par\par \b \uc2  Russian rouble ticks up but seen easing in September. \b0\par\par\uc2 458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2000\par 12:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MOSCOW, Aug 31 (Reuters) - The Russian rouble edged up on Thursday, supported by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar sales and demand for roubles to pad end-of-month balance sheets, but dealers forecast the rouble would weaken in September. \par \par \uc2  An unexpected rise in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves also supported the rouble, but was not expected to offset the predicted rise in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The rouble's weighted average for today settlement at a unified trading session of eight Russian exchanges, usually tightly controlled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ticked up to 27.7531 per dollar from 27.7547 per dollar on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  However, the rouble's gain was small and based as usual on the results of the morning session, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its official next day rate unchanged at 27.75 per dollar . \par \par \uc2  "The volume fell because banks were afraid to buy dollars as they need to close their balances at the end of the month," said Guta-Bank dealer Vladimir Kotyatov. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily recently to counter aggressive demand for hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold about $50 million, less than on recent days, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Trade volume in the morning session fell to $195.91 million, higher than average, from a very strong $242.39 million on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expected the rouble to fall as soon as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped selling dollars in order to replenish its reserves. Dollar demand was also set to resume in early September, as it usually does at the start of the month, they said. \par \par \uc2  A rise in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which surprised the market after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would stabilise the nervous market. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which shrunk in the first half of August, rose to $24.1 billion on August 25 from $23.4 billion a week earlier. \par \par \uc2  "It's positive news and the market welcomed it. It will help to smooth the dollar rise, but won't reverse the upward (dollar) trend," said Nikolai Polunin, a dealer at Dialog Optim bank. \par \par \uc2  He said dollar demand would be sustained because the market expected an increase in federal budget spending after a series of catastrophes, including the sinking of the Kursk submarine and a fire which burned Moscow's television tower, the highest man-made structure in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Kotyatov said dollar demand would be spurred by fears of rising consumer prices inflation, based on higher petrol prices, and the rouble could lose up to 20 kopecks against the dollar within the next week, if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed it. \par \par \uc2  Roubles for tomorrow settlement were little changed by 0950 GMT at 27.7800/27.7900 after a previous average of 27.7853 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw8v09j01\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
