\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Kazakh economy seen weathering tenge storm. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mike Collett-White \par \uc2 691 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 1999\par 15:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ALMATY, April 5 (Reuters) - Kazakhstan's decision to follow Russia's example and effectively devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may send tremors through the economy but they will probably pass more quickly than those shaking its giant neighbour, officials and economists said. \par \par \uc2  Kazakhstan, the latest victim of the economic crisis in Russia, on Monday abandoned its increasingly costly defence of the tenge in favour of a free floating rate. \par \par \uc2  A hint of panic crept into the markets within hours, with the tenge losing 13 percent of its value against the dollar in extremely thin trade. \par \par \uc2  But while the new policy may come as a jolt to the Central Asian state's 16 million people, it need not spell the same kind of disaster as a similar move last August did for Russia. \par \par \uc2  "The change will come as a shock over the next couple of weeks before a return to normality," said Madina Dushimova, research director at KazkommertsSecurities, part of Kazakhstan's largest private bank. \par \par \uc2  "The fundamentals are different for Kazakhstan than they were for Russia. There is no treasury-bill pyramid, not the same huge foreign debt, fiscal discipline here is much better and, most importantly, there is a government and a strong national bank," she added. \par \par \uc2  Keen to stress the distance from Moscow, Finance Minister Uraz Dzhandosov said that there would be no Russian-style debt defaults or moratoria and that the Kazakh banking system was better prepared to withstand the inevitable strain. \par \par \uc2  Pressure has grown on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to act to stave off increasing competition from neighbouring countries, particularly key trade partner Russia, where the rouble has lost more than 75 percent of its value since August. \par \par \uc2  Resource-rich Kazakhstan, five times the size of France, has also suffered from the slump in prices for key export earners oil and metals. Its trade deficit has widened and foreign reserves have been whittled away. \par \par \uc2  Kazakhstan's first course of action was to support the tenge through regular \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ban imports of some goods from \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and impose a whopping 200 percent import duty on goods from Central Asian states Uzbekistan and Kyrgyzstan. \par \par \uc2  But Prime Minister Nurlan Balgimbayev said in a statement that the trade barriers had not worked and ran counter to Kazakhstan's policy of opening up its economy. \par \par \uc2  "To close the borders is economically virtually impossible and incorrect from a political point of view," Balgimbayev said. "Russia and our Central Asian neighbours are friends and it is not worth getting involved in trade wars." \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Dzhandosov said the tenge would now be allowed to find its natural level in the hope that this will help producers and exporters to compete more effectively. \par \par \uc2  The bold move, which took many bankers in the commercial centre of Almaty by surprise, was broadly welcomed, if only because the old policies were clearly not working. \par \par \uc2  "The moment arrived where the price of supporting the tenge was too high," said Otbert de Jong, general manager of ABN Amro Bank Kazakhstan. "The decision was better than not doing anything at all," Dushimova said. \par \par \uc2  Kazakh producers and exporters will welcome the decision, although there is a price to pay in the form of a new rule requiring that all companies sell half their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  export earnings on to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chairman Kadyrzhan Damitov said that, in an attempt to avoid mass bank deposit withdrawals, savers were to be allowed to convert accounts into dollars at Friday's exchange rate of 88.3 tenge to the U.S. dollar if they did not withdraw for nine months. The tenge fell to 100.0 per dollar on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Companies will be able to convert 30 percent of their tenge deposits if they keep them in the bank for six months. \par \par \uc2  Pension funds with deposits held in tenge-denominated treasury bills will be allowed to change them for five-year hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills. Compulsory reserve requirements for commercial banks have also been reduced to five percent from 10 percent. ((Almaty Newsroom, +7 3272 509410 moscow.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv4503k9j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
