\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Primakov seeks unity to fight Russia's chaos. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gareth Jones \par \uc2 871 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 1998\par 19:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MOSCOW, Sept 14 (Reuters) - Prime Minister Yevgeny Primakov struggled on Monday to forge a broad-based government to tackle Russia's economic mayhem and suffered an early setback when several key political forces said they would not join his team. \par \par \uc2  As both Communists and liberals distanced themselves from him, Primakov confirmed a shift of policy from strict monetarism towards social welfare and industrial growth in comments likely to worry Western lenders meeting in London to discuss Russia. \par \par \uc2  President Boris Yeltsin, severely weakened by the financial crisis, remained at his country residence. Primakov was due to brief him on Tuesday in the Kremlin on his cabinet plans. \par \par \uc2  "This is a national government, a patriotic government, which must concern itself with the interests of Russia and her people," Primakov told the cabinet, still largely composed of acting ministers from the outgoing administration. \par \par \uc2  The former foreign minister, approved by parliament on Friday, faces a tough task to try to restore order to Russia's chaotic finances and to regain the trust of international investors dismayed by its default on some foreign debt. \par \par \uc2  He has vowed to push on with reforms but wants to cushion their impact on the most vulnerable parts of society. \par \par \uc2  "We cannot conduct reforms which reflect badly among the people," Primakov said as he chaired his first cabinet meeting. \par \par \uc2  But despite his pitch, the Communists and their parliamentary allies, who all supported his candidacy, said they would not propose new candidates to join the government. \par \par \uc2  Their statement also called for stepping up preparations for a nationwide protest on October 7 that would call for Yeltsin's resignation and an early presidential election. \par \par \uc2  Grigory Yavlinsky, leader of the pro-reform Yabloko party which also voted for Primakov, similarly refused to join the cabinet, saying his economic views differed too sharply from those of First Deputy Prime Minister Yuri Maslyukov. \par \par \uc2  Maslyukov, a moderate Communist, is now in broad charge of economic policy and has said \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will pursue a more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy aimed at reviving domestic industry. \par \par \uc2  "It would be wrong to turn the government into a debating club," Yavlinsky told reporters after meeting Primakov. \par \par \uc2  This means that of the eight parties or groupings in the State Duma, the lower house of parliament, all but three have ruled out taking part in what Primakov has said should be a non-partisan government reflecting a wide spectrum of opinions. \par \par \uc2  On Monday Maslyukov said his top priority would be paying off wage and pension arrears - a chronic problem affecting millions of Russians including soldiers, doctors and teachers. \par \par \uc2  Interfax news agency quoted Primakov, 68, as saying he could not rule out "extraordinary measures" to clear the wage backlog. \par \par \uc2  It was unclear what measures he had in mind but Western economists and Russian liberals fear he might start printing money - a move which could spark hyperinflation. \par \par \uc2  Acting Finance Minister Mikhail Zadornov, a liberal, said he hoped Russia would avoid printing its way out of the crisis. \par \par \uc2  "We would prefer not to rush towards \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  credits," Interfax quoted Zadornov as saying. "Hopefully it will be possible to get by without an emission." \par \par \uc2  Maslyukov, who once headed the Soviet state planning organisation Gosplan, has said he will have an economic programme ready by the end of the week. \par \par \uc2  His proposals are sure to be carefully scrutinised by Western countries and global lenders like the International Monetary Fund (IMF) which have told Moscow it can only expect further financial help if it sticks to tough market reforms. \par \par \uc2  U.S. President Bill Clinton said Russia must reaffirm its commitment to democracy and free markets. \par \par \uc2  "If Russia is willing to take these steps, we must do everything we can to provide support," he said. \par \par \uc2  Senior foreign and finance ministry officials from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations met in London on Monday to discuss Russia and reached similar conclusions to Clinton. \par \par \uc2  Russian Deputy Foreign Minister Georgy Mamedov told reporters after briefing the G7 officials that a team of IMF experts was likely to visit Moscow later this week. \par \par \uc2  Russia is keen to get its hands on another tranche of the $22.6 billion rescue package agreed with the IMF in July. \par \par \uc2  In some sorely needed good news for the beleaguered government, Russian markets appeared calmer on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , now headed by Soviet-era banker Viktor Gerashchenko, set its official rouble rate for Tuesday at 8.6707 to the dollar, up from Monday's level of 11.4281. The battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly hit around 20 roubles to the dollar last week. However, analysts said the rouble rebound may be short-lived. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first deputy chairman, Sergei Aleksashenko, followed former chairman Sergei Dubinin by resigning and said he had a different approach to Gerashchenko. \par \par \uc2  After Monday's cabinet meeting, Primakov consulted former premier Viktor Chernomyrdin. Chernomyrdin, Yeltsin's favoured candidate for prime minister, stepped aside last week to clear the way for Primakov after being rejected twice by the Duma. \par \par \uc2  Chernomyrdin, who served as premier from 1992 until his sacking in March this year, also reiterated his intention to run for the presidency in the next election due in mid-2000. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9e010er\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
