\par\par \b \uc2  WALL ST WEEK AHEAD/Is the bull market over? \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Melville \par \uc2 915 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 1998\par 19:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 30 (Reuters) - The world's woes have washed ashore for U.S. investors, ruining summer vacations and casting into doubt the health, if not the life, of the bull market. \par \par \uc2  The global tumult, climaxed by Russia's near meltdown last week, has produced widespread wreckage on Wall Street. Last week, the Dow Jones Industrial Average tumbled 481.97 points, its largest weekly point loss in history. \par \par \uc2  While leading Russian politicians, led by acting Prime Minister Viktor Chernomyrdin, reached a compromise Sunday aimed at halting Russia's crisis, that development may have litte impact on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  Most major U.S. stock indexes are now well into double-digit loss territory since peaking in July. The Dow and Standard & Poor's 500 index have each skidded more than 13 percent and the Nasdaq Composite more than 18 percent. \par \par \uc2  Another grim statistic: Nearly four out of five stocks listed on the New York Stock Exchange are trading below their 10-week and 30-week moving averages. \par \par \uc2  Those numbers put the market well into correction territory and have many on Wall Street talking bluntly about the prospects of a market other than the bull variety. \par \par \uc2  "The odds are increasing we are going to have a serious bear market," Morgan Stanley Dean Witter global strategist Barton Biggs said during a Friday interview on CNBC television. \par \par \uc2  While definitions of a bear market vary, most use 20 percent as the threshhold, meaning Nasdaq is already in the ballpark. But that simple definition falls short for some. \par \par \uc2  "There are plenty of people who are using that phrase," said Charlie Crane, chief market strategist at Key Asset Management. "I don't think we're in one. The thing for me that distinguishes a true bear market from a nasty correction is duration." \par \par \uc2  Which is not to say Crane is bullish. Rather, he said, he "prematurely" called the end of the bull market last year and now views a 20 percent correction - such as the market experienced in 1987, just a bit slower - as a possibility. \par \par \uc2  The bears have more company than at any point in the past three years. Recent sentiment surveys are extremely bleak, with just 22 percent of respondents expressing a bullish outlook in the last weekly survey by the American Association of Individual Investors. Bears claimed a 32 percent share of the investor market. \par \par \uc2  And yet, even with fear apparently trouncing greed in the sentiment market, some bulls are holding fast, Goldman Sachs U.S. strategist Abby Joseph Cohen, for example. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, Cohen again stated her view that stocks were undervalued and predicted the Dow, which ended the week a quiet day's decline from 8,000, would finish 1998 well above 9,300. \par \par \uc2  "We conclude that problems of great consequence to other nations have been largely responsible for recent declines in U.S. stock prices, and that these declines are notably out of proportion with the likely impact on the U.S. economy," Cohen said in a note to clients. \par \par \uc2  Cohen is not alone in her view. Brown Brothers Harriman market analyst Charles Blood also said recent events had not convinced him to alter his expectations for an upturn, albeit a somewhat delayed one. \par \par \uc2  "Nothing has changed about our outlook," he said. "We've done a lot of damage here but I think the background is still okay." \par \par \uc2  One reason the domestic story is taking a back seat to international events is a lack of fresh U.S. news. Wall Street is between earnings seasons and last week offered a dearth of domestic economic news to digest. \par \par \uc2  This Friday, the market is slated to receive the one report that in the past has proven capable of unilaterlly turning markets - the monthly payrolls report. \par \par \uc2  For the first time in years, analysts will scrutinise the data not for its inflationary impact - inflation has been all but disregarded as a threat by many - but for any weakness that may free the hand of the Federal Reserve to lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Few on Wall Street expect such an easing anytime soon, however, as the Fed has given little sign it would consider such a move. \par \par \uc2  The most recent Fed comments on rates were offered on Friday by St. Louis Fed President William Poole, who would go only so far as to say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take a "wait-and-see" approach on rates. \par \par \uc2  The recent conclusion of the General Motors strike will also cloud the jobs numbers, making it difficult to pin any policy hopes on the report. \par \par \uc2  In the absence of news at home, investors have fixed on the dismal news from abroad. \par \par \uc2  A stream of bad news that started with last summer's economic woes in Southeast Asia has swelled to flood levels, sweeping through the economies in Russia and Latin America. \par \par \uc2  The global malaise has sent commodity prices slumping, which in turn has hit some developed economies like Canada and Australia, both of which have felt the effects in slumping \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Canada resorted to raising interest rates to protect its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Hong Kong directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in equity markets as a defensive measure and \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  all but blocked rouble trading, all efforts analysts generally argued were doomed to failure. \par \par \uc2  "Authorities around the world are bungling," Morgan Stanley's Biggs said. "These aren't catastrophes but Canada, Hong Kong and Russia are all negatives." \par \par \uc2  ((-Wall Street desk, 212-859-1730)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8u01swq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
