\par\par \b \uc2  World Mkt Turmoil At A Glance: Russia Spooks Tokyo Stocks \b0\par\par\uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 05:07\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- Tokyo stocks are sharply lower on growing worries of\par Russia-led global market turmoil and concerns about delay in\par passage of financial stabilization bills. The Nikkei is down\par 405.99 points, or 2.7%, at 14,460.04. The Nikkei's lowest\par close for the decade is 14,309.41 in August 1992.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- The Australian dollar touched a post-1983-float low of\par US$0.5605 in early trade, before recovering to US$0.5632.\par  Australian stocks have\par followed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lower, off 48.7 points, or 1.9%, at\par 2559.3.\par    -- Large-capitalization stocks will lead U.S. equities on\par a substantial rally out of their recent lows, according to\par Stanley Druckenmiller, chief investment officer for Soros\par Fund Management. In an interview on CNBC Wednesday, he said\par global economic turmoil could prompt a change from the\par Federal Reserve on interest rate policy.\par    Druckenmiller also said Russia's impact on financial\par markets here is "over and done with."He predicted a Russian\par political shakeup, saying that "Yeltsin is clearly out," the\par victim of a "soft coup."\par    -- Major U.S. stock market averages lessened their\par declines late Wednesday after Druckenmiller's comments about\par the Fed. The Dow Jones Industrial Average lost 79.30, or\par 0.92%, to close at 8523.35, as the market took back the\par ground gained in the two preceding sessions.\par    -- \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday said it would stop\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the ruble against the dollar, and\par cancelled dollar trades on the Moscow Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par Exchange. The ruble crashed against the Deutsche mark,\par falling 69% to 7.6 rubles per mark. That implies a rate of\par nearly 14 rubles to the dollar - well below a limit of 9.5\par per dollar that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week set as the floor\par for the current year.\par    -- Citicorp said that because of deteriorating conditions\par in Russia and continued global market volatility it was\par adopting a "more conservative" emerging market debt\par portofolio. It lowered the weight of Brazil, Argentina and\par Ecuador to "underweight" because these countries fell into\par the "high risk" category. Citibank had previously weighted\par Argentina and Ecuador "overweight"; while Brazil was "light\par overweight."\par    -- The Venezulan bolivar closed weaker Wednesday against\par the dollar in heavy trading after news that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par of Venezuela declared its weekly TEM bill auction deserted,\par triggering a dollar-buying spree, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  08-26-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2307EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1141 4223 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du8r022vz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
