\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-World urges Moscow to finally take charge. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 20:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Aug 26 (Reuters) - The world's top economic powers told Russia on Wednesday it alone held the key to rescuing its ravaged economy and rouble \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , urging Moscow to finally get a grip on its staggering financial problems. \par \par \uc2  "There are no shortcuts in restoring market confidence, and the next steps are up to the Russians," White House deputy spokesman Barry Toiv told reporters in Edgartown, Mass., where U.S. President Bill Clinton was on vacation. \par \par \uc2  "Now more than ever it's critical that they get their fiscal house in order and establish policies on the rouble, on government debt and the banking system that will lead Russia back to financial stability," he said. \par \par \uc2  Toiv was speaking as fears were growing in world markets over Russia's economic fate. The rouble dropped more than 40 percent against the German mark on Wednesday, prompting the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to declare it could no longer afford to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost its ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Washington's harsh line was echoed by its key partners in Europe, which put the responsibility for pulling Russia back from the financial abyss squarely on Moscow's shoulders. \par \par \uc2  "Russia must do it by itself," German Finance Minister Theo Waigel said in Germany. \par \par \uc2  In Paris, French President Jacques Chirac urged Moscow to push through economic reforms, saying international support would only work if reforms were implemented decisively by the new government of acting prime minister Viktor Chernomyrdin. \par \par \uc2  The British Treasury said it was closely monitoring the Russia crisis and was in contact with other members of the Group of Seven (G7) top industrial nations. \par \par \uc2  But there appeared to be no plans for a meeting by G7 leaders to discuss the crisis. "There's no plan that I'm aware of for any such meeting," Toiv said. \par \par \uc2  European sources said the four European members of the G7 - Germany, France, Britain and Italy - were in close contact but it was premature to speculate on any joint initiative. \par \par \uc2  Clinton is scheduled to travel to Moscow next week. He is expected to be joined by Deputy U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, the administration's top trouble-shooter when it comes to problems on the international financial scene. \par \par \uc2  Russia in July clinched a $22.6 billion rescue package with the International Monetary Fund and other international lenders aimed at resolving a deep economic crisis that jolted financial markets across the globe. \par \par \uc2  But conditions deteriorated dramatically since that agreement was reached. The rouble plunged to new lows against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a sense of panic spread among ordinary Russians, many of whom stood in long lines to withdraw their savings. \par \par \uc2  "If Russia can take the significant steps that they need to take, they can experience a recovery," Toiv said. "But it's not going to be easy and nobody ever suggested it would be." \par \par \uc2  Even as the United States and top European nations all but ruled out providing fresh funds for Russia, the IMF's top official, managing director Michel Camdessus, rushed off for a set of emergency talks with Russian and Ukrainian leaders. \par \par \uc2  An IMF spokesman said Camdessus would meet Chernomyrdin and Ukrainian President Leonid Kuchma in the Ukraine to discuss "recent developments" in Russia and the fallout on the region. \par \par \uc2  The German government ruled out granting any more international financial aid to Russia, on top of the bailout package put together in July, until Moscow moves to implement agreed tax and budget reforms. Germany is Russia's biggest foreign lender and investor. \par \par \uc2  Russia stunned world markets a week ago by effectively devaluing the rouble and declaring a 90-day moratorium on some of its foreign debt repayments. President Boris Yeltsin then fired the entire government of Prime Minister Sergei Kiriyenko and named Chernomyrdin, whom he had fired from that very job in March, as acting prime minister. \par \par \uc2  Waigel said Germany had good relations with Chernomyrdin, and would "make use of these relations to help Russia." \par \par \uc2  France's Chirac said the international community should back Camdessus's efforts to ease the crisis. But he said, "His \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  today in \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the full support of the G7, will only succeed if the essential domestic reforms sought by President Yeltsin are implemented resolutely by the new government of Chernomyrdin." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01jug\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
